  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe are to minimize the maximum overlap of a step function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with its complement 1-h. The previous implementation used binary search and continuous optimization, but we need to find a better solution. The new approach will use four initialization conditions: a comb-like function, a circumcircle-like function, a binary \"Mexican hat\" function, and a rotated fill function. We'll then refine each using AdaptivePSO (Adaptive Particle Swarm Optimization) from the `pyswarman` library. This hybrid of pattern-based initializations and a parameter-adaptive PSO search is chosen to avoid local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nOur state space is discrete binary vectors in {0,1}^n with exactly n/2 ones. The optimization objective is to minimize the maximum correlation over all shifts of the discrete convolution:\n```\nmin h_max(time) = max_k c[k] * dx\nwhere dx = 2.0 / n\nc = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n```\nMaintaining feasibility is automatic since flipping a bit preserves the total sum of ones. The PSO algorithm will be formulated with:\n- Decision variables: binary vector h \u2208 {0,1}^n\n- Fitness = time * max_correlation (penalizing slow improvement)\n- Particle dimension: n (vector size)\n- Inertia factor: dynamically adjusted based on neighborhood\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization\nUse four distinct generation methods:\n1. Combs: spread ones evenly but avoid symmetry\n2. Circumaround: manufactured pattern avoiding exact circularity\n3. MexicanHat: central density of ones thickening toward each edge\n4. RotatedFill: randomly thin out earlier patterns while maintaining sums\n\nb. PSO parameterization\nEach particle:\n- Position: vector of binary responses\n- Velocity: vector of float mutations\n- Personal best: tracking locally better solutions\n- Global best: common improvement across optimizations\n\nc. Fitness evaluation\nFor each candidate solution:\n1. Project to feasible space (ensuring sum=n/2)\n2. Compute full correlation:\n   ```\n   corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n   fitness = max(corr) * (2.0/n)\n   fitness_penalty = (time_so_far * 0.5) * (1 - (current_temp/temp_peak))\n   final_fitness = fitness + 0.0001*time_consumed\n   ```\n \nd. Update mechanism\nParticle updates include:\n- Velocity:\n   ```\n   velocity = w*velocity + c1*r1*(pbest - particle) + c2*r2*(gbest - particle)\n   particle = particle + velocity\n   ```\n- Binary rounding for positions\n- Temperature scaling for premature convergence detection\n\ne. Failure handling\nRestore feasibility via iterative projection (as in previous implementation) if constraints break. The solver variant is discrete with continuous mutation accepted.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\nThe default search will use:\n- Generation method: Circumaround\n- PSO parameters: w=0.8, c1=1.2, c2=1.2\n- Neighborhood size: n/2\n\nThe variations we'll explore:\n- Different problem dimensionality (n=250) compatible with multiple scales\n- Higher crossover rate between neighborhood methods\n- Adaptive cooling schedules for stagnating solutions\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nWe'll allocate:\n- 0.25s/min for initial pattern generation\n- 0.7 for optimization\n- Identify stagnation: if fitness doesn't improve for 35 generations\n\nThe fallback is a low-cost repair projection. We'll time each optimization.\n\n6. Validation and acceptance\nFor each returned vector:\n- Check feasible: all elements in [0,1] and sum = n/2\n- Revalidate with quadruple precision correlation\n- Ensure timedelta does not exceed budget",
